cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "rigid: a text-first, content-addressed language kernel"
message: "If you use this software or refer to its results, please cite it."
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Dudas
    given-names: David
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0000-9348-5438"
repository-code: "https://github.com/DavidRDudas/rigidlang"
license: MPL-2.0
version: "1.8.1"
abstract: >-
  rigid derives an exact reference graph as a byproduct of compilation. A
  definition's identity is the SHA-256 of its canonical form, with bound names
  erased and every reference replaced by the hash of what it references, so
  names are metadata and a rename cannot silently change meaning. Unlike prior
  content-addressed systems it keeps ordinary text files and Git, and bounds
  dynamism with declared dispatch sets rather than banning it. The exact graph
  makes several things decidable that are elsewhere approximate: whether a diff
  changed any definition's meaning, whether a merge conflict is real, whether a
  dependency gained a capability, what code is unreachable, and which prior
  conclusions a change invalidates.
keywords:
  - programming languages
  - content addressing
  - compilers
  - software evolution
  - supply chain security
